        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the classification for <m>Q_1</m> using <m>K=1</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>K=1</m>: C
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the classification for <m>Q_1</m> using <m>K=3</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>K=3</m>: C
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>



        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the classification for <m>Q_2</m> using <m>K=1</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>K=1</m>: C
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the classification for <m>Q_2</m> using <m>K=3</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>K=3</m>: B
                </p>
            </answer>
        </task>


    </exercise>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Tradeoff</title>

        <sidebyside widths="30% 33% 30%">
            <!-- <figure> -->
                <image source="figures/Fig2-16a.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A plot of two classes (yellow circles and blue circles)
                        in the plane.
                        The KNN boundary is plotted in solid black, while Bayes
                        decision boundary is plotted in purple.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            <!-- </figure> -->

            <!-- <figure> -->
                <image source="figures/Fig2-15.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A plot of two classes (yellow circles and blue circles)
                        in the plane.
                        The KNN boundary is plotted in solid black, while Bayes
                        decision boundary is plotted in purple.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            <!-- </figure> -->

            <!-- <figure> -->
                <image source="figures/Fig2-16c.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        A plot of two classes (yellow circles and blue circles)
                        in the plane.
                        The KNN boundary is plotted in solid black, while Bayes
                        decision boundary is plotted in purple.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            <!-- </figure> -->
        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Purple is Bayes decision boundary.
                        Same on both.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>K=1</m>, boundary is overly flexible, finds patterns
                        not there.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>K=100</m> not enough flexibility.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>More on tradeoff</title>

        <sidebyside widths="60% 40%">
            <figure>
                <image source="figures/Fig2-17.png">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Training error rate (plotted in solid blue) and Test
                        error rate (plotted in solid orange) versus 1/K.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </figure>

            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Right side, <m>K=1</m>, training is 0 error but high
                            test error.
                        </p>
                    </li>

                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Test error has same U shape as bias-variance
                            tradeoff in regression setting: decline at first,
                            then increase again when overfitting.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

